# Day 4 — CSS Box Model + Layout

## Learning Objectives
By the end of this day, you will be able to:
- Understand the CSS box model completely
- Control element display and positioning
- Use flexbox for modern layouts
- Apply box model properties effectively

## The CSS Box Model
Every element in HTML is a box. The box model consists of:

```css
div {
  content;    /* The actual content */
  padding;    /* Space inside the border */
  border;     /* Border around the padding */
  margin;     /* Space outside the border */
}
```

### Box Model Components
1. **Content** — the actual content (text, images)
2. **Padding** — space between content and border
3. **Border** — border around padding
4. **Margin** — space outside the border

```css
.box {
  width: 200px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 2px solid black;
  margin: 20px;
}
```

### Box-Sizing Property
```css
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
```
- **content-box** (default) — width/height only apply to content
- **border-box** — width/height include padding and border

## Display Property
Controls how an element is displayed:
- **block** — starts on a new line (div, p, h1)
- **inline** — flows with text (span, a)
- **inline-block** — like inline but has width/height
- **none** — hides the element
- **flex** — enables flexbox layout
- **grid** — enables grid layout

```css
.inline-block {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
}
```

## Position Property
Controls how an element is positioned:
- **static** — default, in document flow
- **relative** — offset from normal position
- **absolute** — positioned relative to nearest positioned ancestor
- **fixed** — positioned relative to viewport
- **sticky** — behaves like relative until scrolled into view

```css
.absolute {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50px;
  left: 50px;
}
```

## Flexbox
Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout model for arranging items.

### Flex Container
```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

### Flex Properties
- **display: flex** — makes container a flex container
- **flex-direction** — row, row-reverse, column, column-reverse
- **justify-content** — horizontal alignment of items
- **align-items** — vertical alignment of items
- **flex-wrap** — wrap items to next line or keep on one line
- **gap** — space between items

### Flex Item Properties
- **flex-grow** — grow to fill available space
- **flex-shrink** — shrink to fit available space
- **flex-basis** — initial size before growing/shrinking
- **order** — controls the order of items

```css
.item {
  flex: 1;
  background-color: lightblue;
}
```

## Common Flexbox Patterns
### Centering Content
```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  height: 100vh;
}
```

### Navigation Bar
```css
.nav {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
}
```

### Card Layout
```css
.cards {
  display: flex;
  gap: 20px;
}
.card {
  flex: 1;
}
```

## Practical Applications
- Navigation bars
- Card grids
- Centering content
- Flexible layouts
- Responsive design foundations

# Day 5 — CSS Grid + Responsive Design

## Learning Objectives
By the end of this day, you will be able to:
- Create CSS Grid layouts with rows and columns
- Use grid placement properties
- Build responsive designs with media queries
- Use fluid units for scalable layouts

## CSS Grid Overview
Grid is a two-dimensional layout system for arranging items in rows and columns. It is more powerful than flexbox for complex layouts.

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr;
  gap: 20px;
}
```

## Grid Container Properties
- **display: grid** — makes element a grid container
- **grid-template-columns** — defines column sizes
- **grid-template-rows** — defines row sizes
- **gap** — space between grid items
- **grid-template-areas** — named areas for complex layouts

## Grid Areas
```css
.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header header header"
    "sidebar content sidebar"
    "footer footer footer";
  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 200px;
  gap: 20px;
}
.header { grid-area: header; }
.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }
.content { grid-area: content; }
.footer { grid-area: footer; }
```

## Grid Placement
- **grid-column** — which column(s) an item spans
- **grid-row** — which row(s) an item spans
- **grid-column-start** — starting column
- **grid-column-end** — ending column
- **grid-row-start** — starting row
- **grid-row-end** — ending row

```css
.item {
  grid-column: 1 / 3;
  grid-row: 2;
}
```

## Auto-Placement
- **auto-flow** — controls how items are placed
- **grid-auto-columns** — size of auto-generated columns
- **grid-auto-rows** — size of auto-generated rows

## Responsive Design
Responsive design makes websites work on all devices (desktop, tablet, mobile).

### Media Queries
```css
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .container {
    flex-direction: column;
  }
}
```

### Common Media Query Breakpoints
- **320px** — small phones
- **480px** — large phones
- **768px** — tablets
- **1024px** — small laptops
- **1200px** — desktops

### Media Query Types
- **max-width** — applies below a width
- **min-width** — applies above a width
- **max-height** — applies below a height
- **min-height** — applies above a height
- **orientation** — landscape or portrait

## Fluid Units
- **%** — percentage of parent
- **vw** — 1% of viewport width
- **vh** — 1% of viewport height
- **em** — relative to font size of element
- **rem** — relative to root font size

```css
.container {
  width: 90%;
  max-width: 1200px;
}
```

## Mobile-First Approach
Design for mobile first, then enhance for larger screens:
```css
.card {
  display: block;
}
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .card {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }
}
```

## Responsive Images
```css
img {
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
```

## Practical Applications
- Website layouts
- Card grids
- Navigation bars
- Portfolio galleries
- Dashboard layouts

# Day 10 — JavaScript Fundamentals

## Learning Objectives
By the end of this day, you will be able to:
- Declare and use variables
- Understand JavaScript data types
- Use operators for calculations and comparisons
- Write conditional statements

## What is JavaScript?
JavaScript is a programming language that makes web pages interactive. It runs in the browser and can manipulate HTML, CSS, and respond to user actions.

```html
<script>
  console.log("Hello, JavaScript!");
</script>
```

## Variables
Variables store data values that can be used and modified.

### var
The original JavaScript keyword. Now largely replaced by let and const.
```javascript
var age = 25;
```

### let
Used for variables that can be reassigned.
```javascript
let score = 10;
score = 20; // allowed
```

### const
Used for variables that cannot be reassigned.
```javascript
const pi = 3.14;
// pi = 3.15; // error!
```

### Variable Naming Rules
- Must start with a letter, $, or _
- Cannot contain spaces
- Case-sensitive (myVar and myvar are different)
- Avoid reserved words (class, function, return)
- Use camelCase for readability

```javascript
let userName = "Alice";
const userAge = 25;
```

## Data Types

### Primitive Types
1. **String** — text
```javascript
let name = "Alice";
```
2. **Number** — integers and decimals
```javascript
let age = 25;
let price = 19.99;
```
3. **Boolean** — true or false
```javascript
let isStudent = true;
```
4. **Undefined** — variable declared but not assigned
```javascript
let x;
// x is undefined
```
5. **Null** — intentional empty value
```javascript
let city = null;
```
6. **Symbol** — unique identifier
```javascript
const id = Symbol("id");
```
7. **BigInt** — large integers
```javascript
let bigNumber = 12345678901234567890n;
```

### Checking Data Types
```javascript
typeof name;    // "string"
typeof age;     // "number"
typeof isStudent; // "boolean"
```

## Operators

### Arithmetic Operators
- **+** — Addition
- **-** — Subtraction
- **\*** — Multiplication
- **/** — Division
- **%** — Modulus (remainder)
- **\*\*** — Exponentiation

### Assignment Operators
- **=** — Assign
- **+=** — Add and assign
- **-=** — Subtract and assign
- **\*=** — Multiply and assign
- **/=** — Divide and assign

### Comparison Operators
- **==** — Equal (after type conversion)
- **===** — Strictly equal (same type and value)
- **!=** — Not equal
- **!==** — Strictly not equal
- **>** — Greater than
- **<** — Less than
- **>=** — Greater than or equal
- **<=** — Less than or equal

### Logical Operators
- **&&** — Logical AND
- **||** — Logical OR
- **!** — Logical NOT

## Conditional Statements

### if Statement
```javascript
let temperature = 30;
if (temperature > 25) {
  console.log("It's hot outside");
}
```

### if...else Statement
```javascript
let age = 16;
if (age >= 18) {
  console.log("You can vote");
} else {
  console.log("You cannot vote yet");
}
```

### if...else if...else Statement
```javascript
let score = 85;
if (score >= 90) {
  console.log("Grade: A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
  console.log("Grade: B");
} else if (score >= 70) {
  console.log("Grade: C");
} else {
  console.log("Grade: F");
}
```

### Ternary Operator
A shorthand for if...else:
```javascript
let age = 20;
let status = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
```

### switch Statement
```javascript
let day = "Monday";
switch (day) {
  case "Monday":
    console.log("Start of the week");
    break;
  case "Friday":
    console.log("Almost the weekend");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("Weekday");
}
```

## Common Beginner Mistakes
- Using = instead of == or ===
- Forgetting to declare variables with let or const
- Mixing up data types in comparisons
- Not using strict equality (===)
- Forgetting braces in conditional statements
- Not understanding operator precedence
